ATTACK 1 — Residual divergence at realistic (non-tiny) target values. My re-run above used
target=(500,300), NOT the shipped test's tiny (1,1) calibration, and found a ~$14.78 residual
where the shipped test (at 1,1) would show only a few cents. Investigated whether this is a
NEW gap in the fix or the SAME pre-existing, disclosed target-accrual-formula mismatch the
manifest explicitly calls out (chart's flat `monthTarget` vs metrics.go's
MonthsBetween-prorated `accrual`, "pre-dating SY4 entirely"). Applied the SAME identical-with/
without-flag test the coordinator established as the standard for this exact question (used at
attempt 3 for the CombinedCumulativeBalance invariant, and again here): re-ran both the
nil-exclusions and the flagged-def variant at target=(500,300) -- BOTH produced the IDENTICAL
diff (-14.784394250513287, to the double-precision digit). Since the divergence is byte-for-
byte identical whether or not any plan exclusion is active, it cannot be attributed to this
task's fix; it is the disclosed, pre-existing, out-of-scope accrual-formula difference,
confirmed by the same methodology this run has already established as dispositive. Not a FAIL
basis -- recorded as an observation: the target-accrual-formula mismatch between the chart and
metrics.go is real and scales with target magnitude (not just "a few cents" at realistic
dollar figures), worth its own backlog ticket, but it predates and is independent of SY4.

ATTACK 2 — Merge composition: HI+flagged overlap row. Overlay probe: a single transaction that
is BOTH Category="Health Insurance" AND matched to a flagged def. At the shipped test's own
calibration scale (tiny target, isolating away Attack 1's confound): Living bar = 0 (correctly
excluded from living, HI-first per LivingOutflows' own ordering), Healthcare bar = 700
(claimed once), chart walk vs metrics walk diff < $0.04. My FIRST attempt at this probe used
target=(100,100) and showed a spurious ~$3.70 "divergence" -- re-verified against Attack 1's
finding and confirmed it was the SAME pre-existing target-formula noise at that target scale,
not a double-count; re-ran at the calibrated tiny target and it vanished. No double-count, no
drop of the overlap row.

ATTACK 3 — Month-key alignment: a living-only month (refund-heavy, zero HI rows) and a
separate HI-only month (zero living rows), verifying the merge-then-GroupByMonth doesn't drop
or duplicate either month's bucket. Overlay probe (calibrated tiny target): chart=[-1498,
-1896], metrics=[-1497.96, -1896.12], diff < $0.04 at both points -- both months correctly
present and correctly valued in the walk. This is structurally guaranteed anyway by reading the
code: `nonExcludedOutflows` is built by CONCATENATING the two already-classified transaction
LISTS first (`append(append([]models.Transaction{}, livingOutflows.Transactions...),
healthcareOutflows.Transactions...)`), then `GroupByMonth()` runs ONCE over the merged list --
there is no separate "union of two months-maps" step that could silently drop a month present
in only one source map. Not a defect.

ATTACK 4 — Residual consumer of the old `livingMonth+hcAmt` sum. Grepped handlers.go directly:
the expression no longer exists anywhere in the file (only a comment referencing it as the
thing NOT to do). The Living bar (`livingValues`) still uses the independent
`math.Abs(monthlyLiving[m].SumAmount())` (per the dispatch note: "the Living BAR value stays
as-is... that part is correct and tested") -- confirmed this is a SEPARATE variable from the
walk's `spend`, never recombined with `hcAmt` anywhere downstream. Not a defect.

ATTACK 5 — Equality test calibration claim. Read plan_exclusions_chart_walk_test.go in full
